WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.540
A secure Kubernetes baseline for .NET starts by treating manifests as part of application security.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:20.520
Use trusted images, review image references, and make labels, ports, probes, volumes, service accounts, resource settings, and runtime controls visible before release.

3
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.120
Run pods with least privilege and non-root runtime where possible.

4
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:35.140
Align pod security settings with platform policy, plan writable paths, separate Secrets and ConfigMaps, and use least-privilege service accounts and RBAC.

5
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.240
Do not give a workload Kubernetes API access unless it needs it.

6
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:51.920
Control ingress, TLS, internal service exposure, NetworkPolicies, readiness, liveness, startup behavior, graceful shutdown, resource requests, resource limits, and backpressure.

7
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:58.260
The application and cluster settings should agree on how work enters, runs, scales, and stops.

8
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.260
Finally, keep production operations safe.

9
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:10.800
Redact logs, govern diagnostics, review supply chain controls, use admission policy where appropriate, plan rollback, and maintain incident response steps.

10
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:17.380
Kubernetes security for .NET is strongest when application developers and platform teams share the same baseline.
